{
  "image_name": "3d284b1bf9d227201112a90b3cf4cf15.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how type I interferons, shown as IFNα/IFNβ, signal through the IFNAR1/IFNAR2 receptor complex at the cell surface to trigger different transcriptional outcomes. Binding to the receptor activates the associated kinases JAK1 and TYK2, which then phosphorylate STAT proteins and send signals toward the nucleus.\n\nThe main message is that the same receptor can drive several distinct gene programs depending on which STAT complexes form. One branch produces the ISGF3 complex, containing STAT1, STAT2, and IRF9, which binds ISRE DNA elements and induces antiviral genes such as OAS and MX1. A second branch forms STAT1 homodimers, which bind GAS elements and promote an inflammatory response, with example genes including IRF1 and CXCL9. A third branch forms STAT3 homodimers that also act at GAS-like sites, but in this case the outcome appears inhibitory: STAT3 is linked to SIN3A and repression of inflammatory pathways.\n\nOverall, the diagram contrasts antiviral versus inflammatory versus anti-inflammatory outputs of type I interferon signaling, emphasizing that downstream STAT choice determines whether the response activates host defense genes or suppresses inflammation.",
